---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

// Share of the overflow loop's unscheduled downtime by cause, June to July 2025.
const causes = [
  { label: 'Device not ready', pct: 47 },
  { label: 'Jams', pct: 33 },
  { label: 'Communication faults', pct: 9 },
  { label: 'Motor faults', pct: 5 },
  { label: 'Emergency stops', pct: 4 },
  { label: 'Everything else', pct: 1 },
];
---

<Base
  title="Inbound bottleneck analysis · David Salazar"
  description="How I used alarm and downtime data to find the cause of an inbound bottleneck at Amazon, and built a proposal with a business case, safety review and rollout plan."
  current="work"
>
  <div class="case">
    <header class="case-head">
      <div class="col">
        <h1 data-reveal>Bottleneck analysis</h1>
        <p class="lede" data-reveal>
          Two months of alarm data showed that one line stopping the whole system was also the
          least reliable. I wrote up a way to fix it.
        </p>
      <div class="brief" data-reveal>
        <p><strong>Problem</strong>A loop meant as a relief valve kept stopping, and every stop backed up the inbound scan system.</p>
        <p><strong>What I did</strong>Analyzed two months of alarm and downtime data and wrote a proposal with safety, cost and a rollout plan.</p>
        <p><strong>Result</strong>A plan projecting 10 to 15% more throughput and a payback in under two months.</p>
      </div>

        <dl class="spec" data-reveal>
          <dt>Where</dt>
          <dd>Amazon fulfillment site, Hazleton PA. Area Manager Intern, June to August 2025.</dd>
          <dt>What</dt>
          <dd>A proposal to reroute the inbound scan system, with data, cost, safety and a rollout plan. Developed with Operations, RME and IT, and presented to cross-functional stakeholders.</dd>
          <dt>My role</dt>
          <dd>I did the analysis, wrote the proposal and built the business case. It was a two-week project.</dd>
          <dt>Note</dt>
          <dd>Details are generalized for confidentiality. Figures are rounded, and the projections come from my own model.</dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section>
      <div class="col">
        <h2 data-reveal>The situation</h2>
        <p data-reveal>
          Inbound cartons go through a scan-and-induct system. It fed directly into an overflow
          recirculation loop that was used as a relief valve, and that caused three problems.
        </p>
        <ul data-reveal>
          <li>The recirculation loop jammed and stopped often, and every time it stopped the scan system backed up behind it.</li>
          <li>The loop was congested at peak volume, when it was needed most.</li>
          <li>A person had to stand at a manual diverter pushing cartons from the scan system into the loop, an indirect role on every shift.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="flow" data-reveal>
          <figure>
            <figcaption>How it worked</figcaption>
            <div class="row">
              <span class="node">Scan system</span><span class="arrow">to</span>
              <span class="node">Manual diverter</span><span class="arrow">to</span>
              <span class="node bad">Overflow loop (stops often)</span>
            </div>
          </figure>
          <figure>
            <figcaption>What I proposed</figcaption>
            <div class="row">
              <span class="node">Scan system</span><span class="arrow">to</span>
              <span class="node">Automatic feeder</span><span class="arrow">to</span>
              <span class="node">Two low-stoppage lines</span>
            </div>
            <div class="row" style="margin-top:0.5rem">
              <span class="node">Overflow loop, recirculation only</span>
            </div>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
      <div class="col">
        <h2 data-reveal>What the data showed</h2>
        <p data-reveal>
          I pulled alarm and downtime records for June and July, the peak period. The overflow loop
          logged about 1,400 alarms and roughly 85 hours of unscheduled downtime in two months.
        </p>
        <p data-reveal>
          Two causes explained most of it, and both repeat. Device-not-ready alarms and jams
          together made up about 80% of the lost time. That mattered because repeatable causes can
          be designed around.
        </p>
        <ul class="bars" data-reveal aria-label="Share of overflow loop downtime by cause">
          {causes.map((c) => (
            <li>
              <span>{c.label}</span>
              <span class="track"><span class="fill" style={`width:${c.pct}%`}></span></span>
              <span class="pct">{c.pct}%</span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <p class="figure-note" data-reveal>Share of the loop's unscheduled downtime by cause, June to July 2025, rounded.</p>
        <p data-reveal>
          I then looked at the two lines I wanted to reroute to. They were mostly used for
          upstacking and were available for most of the shift. Over the same two months they logged
          about 200 alarms and roughly 11 hours of downtime, about 87% less downtime than the
          overflow loop, and the alarms were simpler. A third pair of lines carrying similar volume
          showed about 56% less downtime than the loop, so volume alone did not explain the loop's
          downtime.
        </p>
        <p data-reveal>
          For a baseline I used average daily carton throughput across all shifts for the same
          period. Every projection in the proposal is measured against that number.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="col">
        <h2 data-reveal>The proposal</h2>
        <div class="runin">
          <p data-reveal>
            <strong>Reroute the scan system to the two reliable lines.</strong> A flexible conveyor
            section would connect the scan system to those lines, and an automatic feeder would
            replace the manual diverter. The overflow loop would go back to being a dedicated
            recirculation loop.
          </p>
          <p data-reveal>
            <strong>Clear the floor space it needs.</strong> During floor walks I noticed pallets parked
            where the new path had to run, so the proposal moved them to a marked staging zone.</p>
          <p data-reveal>
            <strong>Start temporary, then make it permanent.</strong> The plan was a temporary
            setup first, tracking the same metrics against the baseline, then a permanent
            installation once the numbers held up.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
      <div class="col">
        <h2 data-reveal>Safety</h2>
        <p data-reveal>
          The manual diverter role carried two risks I could point to, so the safety case sat next
          to the cost case.
        </p>
        <ul data-reveal>
          <li><strong>Ergonomics.</strong> Repetitive side-to-side pushing for long stretches, with injuries already seen in that role at the site.</li>
          <li><strong>Hands near fast conveyors.</strong> The loop runs fast enough that associates pushing cartons could have their hands caught by oncoming boxes. An automatic feeder removes the manual pushing altogether.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="col">
        <h2 data-reveal>Cost and benefit</h2>
        <p data-reveal>
          The equipment was a flexible conveyor section and a feeder, a small cost next to the
          labor it replaced. On my estimates the payback was under two months. I projected a 10 to
          15% increase in throughput from less downtime, by lifting the scan system from the
          baseline, and I counted the removed indirect role as the labor saving.
        </p>
        <p data-reveal>
          These are projections. They depend on the overflow loop's downtime no longer reaching the
          scan system, and I labeled them that way in the proposal.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
      <div class="col">
        <h2 data-reveal>Working with other teams</h2>
        <p data-reveal>
          The plan listed the approvals it needed up front: Operations and RME sign-off, equipment
          quotes from RME, and a final safety check. I developed it with input
          from Operations, RME and IT, and I presented it to cross-functional stakeholders.</p>
        <div class="actions" data-reveal>
          <a class="btn" href="/#work">Back to work</a>
          <a class="textlink" href="/work/capstone/">Next: Capstone Challenge</a>
        <a class="textlink" href="/#contact">Get in touch</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
